From cec16c708eac3d14888dc5cadb72e6119a82a7c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <engineering@projectluma.org>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 17:47:09 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] screen-cast: Crop window streams to the window frame

A window stream's buffer is the window's whole surface and its video crop
was the buffer bounds, so captures carried whatever the client draws
outside its frame: the shadow margin of client-side decorations (GTK,
libadwaita, Chromium and Electron) and the invisible resize borders of
Mutter's X11 frames. A consumer that takes an opaque format turns those
translucent pixels black, so a captured window sat in a dark band.

The crop is now the frame rectangle relative to the buffer origin, in
buffer pixels: the offset of the frame in the buffer rectangle and its
size, times the texture's buffer pixels per stage unit (buffer scale,
fractional scale through a viewport, Xwayland scaling). It is computed for
every recorded frame, so it follows resizes, and it is the whole buffer
for maximized, tiled and fullscreen windows that draw no margin.
Window streams already offer BGRA (DRM ARGB8888) ahead of BGRx, so a
consumer that accepts alpha gets transparent rounded corners.

Memory (SHM) streams drew the window through meta_window_actor_get_image(),
at the actor's resource scale, which at fractional scales differs from the
buffer's: at 150% a GTK 4 window was drawn at twice its buffer size and the
crop, in buffer pixels, cut it short. They now draw through the same blit
as DMA-BUF streams and read it back, so both paths and the crop share one
coordinate space.

A frame recorded after a commit that resizes the window but before the
stage's relayout painted the new buffer into the old allocation. The blit
now brings the surface actor's allocation up to date first.
